.live-spec/checkpoints/

# Python's own compiled bytecode cache. Never tracked, but present on disk the moment anything
# imports a .py file — `git grep --untracked` (tests/test_dialog_warning_guard.py's own
# one-file-in-the-tree proof, PLAN q-581) picks up an untracked file's bytes even when git
# otherwise ignores the path by convention, so a stray .pyc embedding the same string constant as
# its source module reads as a second copy of the rule. Reproduced live on CI, 2026-09-02: this
# line was simply missing.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# a heavy-byte surface's rendered bytes (SPEC E-7, doc-order-generated.md Requirement 247,
# criterion 4) live outside git; only its manifest line and hash travel under git
.live-spec/snapshot/blobs/

# outbox/ holds private upstream notes (feedback-collector) — never tracked, never pushed
outbox/
.live-spec-fence
.claude/worktrees/

# A page the document renderer produced is built for one reading and is cleared to the attic, so
# it is never committed; the attic keeps it on disk, recoverable, and its manifest line is
# tracked (SPEC INV-286).
*.html

# A page nothing regenerates is the artifact itself and is tracked: a frozen norm card, a
# hand-built decision page, a test fixture. The sweep clears every render before a commit, so
# these lines only ever admit an artifact. A page inside the fenced prototype home is named by
# no line here — the fence gate reads a prod file for a fenced path (SPEC INV-17), and the ones
# already tracked stay tracked.
!docs/norms/*.html
!docs/decisions/*.html
!tests/fixtures/**/*.html
.claude/

# spec-freeze working baselines (regenerable from the pre-rewrite commit)
.spec-freeze/

# 2.0 movement working inventory (survives /clear; regenerable)
.2.0-work/

# A session extract holds the person's own turns pulled out of a transcript, which is private
# conversation. It belongs in a scratch directory and never in this repository (SPEC INV-302), so a
# run that names its output here leaves an ignored file rather than a committed one.
session-extract-*.md

# A copy kept beside a file before it was rewritten is a safety net for one working session, and the
# repository's own history is the durable record of what a file used to hold. Committing one leaves a
# second copy of a live source in the tree, which then drifts and gets read as if it still governed —
# guardrails/language-rules.json.bak-pre-langsweep still stated a threshold the rule home had dropped
# (caught 2026-08-04). These stay on disk, recoverable, and out of the repository.
*.bak
*.bak-*


# product-prover is an external skill (github.com/happysasha18/product-prover),
# installed by scripts/install-external-skills.sh — the clone is never tracked here
skills/product-prover/

# text-audit is an external skill too, extracted 2026-08-18 (github.com/happysasha18/text-audit,
# adapter at skills/text-audit-pack/SKILL.md). scripts/install-external-skills.sh does not fetch it
# yet — that installer is still single-skill and its multi-skill extension is unclaimed pack work — so
# a manual clone under this path is the only way it lands here today. This line just keeps such a
# clone out of the repository, the same as product-prover's.
skills/text-audit/
